<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Erythrae</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.) · capital Erythrae</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ionian city on the peninsula facing Chios, home of a famous Sibyl and member of the twelve-city Ionian League.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Erythrae stood on the coast of the peninsula opposite <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chios">Chios</a>, founded by tradition by Cnopus, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codrus">Codrus of Athens</a>, with mixed settlers c. 1000 BCE. It joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ionian-league">Ionian League</a> and, as an early rival of Chios, fought several wars with the island.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erythraean-sibyl">Erythraean Sibyl</a>, a prophetess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, gave the city renown. Erythrae was governed by aristocracies and later oligarchies and democracies, and sent eight ships to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade">Battle of Lade</a> in 494 BCE. An Athenian decree of the 450s BCE imposed a democratic constitution and garrison on it as a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a> member.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Erythrae revolted from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> in 412 BCE and later swung between Persian, Athenian and Macedonian influence. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> it was a free city, allied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>, and passed into the province of Asia after 133 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:39.80%;width:17.25%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Erythrae</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Erythrai</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-566&amp;focus=erythrae" aria-label="Erythrae on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5338 799 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Erythrae lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5658 1055L6140 1251M5658 1055L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5658 1055h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5658 1055h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-566&amp;focus=erythrae">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

